    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this section, we introduce the deﬁnition of the model of interest along with the relevant notation. We then proceed to state the main results of this work."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H2",
    "id" : 3,
    "level" : "Subtitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.08, 644.379, 286.236, 658.74 ],
    "heading level" : 2,
    "font" : "CMBX12",
    "font size" : 14.346,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2.1 Deﬁnition of the model"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.079, 533.903, 510.218, 626.467 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Throughout, we use the notation N := {1, 2, . . .}, N0 := N∪{0} and [n] := {1, . . ., n} for each n ∈ N. We consider a labeled directed graph (V, E) with vertex set V = [n] for some n ∈ N and edge set E ⊂ {(v, w): v, w ∈ V }. The natural number representing a vertex is then referred to as its label. In such a graph, a sequence of vertices (v1, v2, . . ., vk+1) is a directed path of length k ∈ N if"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 240.599, 501.143, 353.895, 521.885 ],
    "font" : "CMMI12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "∀i ∈ [k]: (vi, vi+1) ∈ E"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 6,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.08, 468.383, 311.254, 489.126 ],
    "font" : "CMBX12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "and an undirected path of length k ∈ N if"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 198.72, 435.623, 396.48, 456.365 ],
    "font" : "CMMI12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "∀i ∈ [k]: (vi, vi+1) ∈ E ∨ (vi+1, vi) ∈ E."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.08, 411.34, 256.692, 423.307 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "A labeled directed graph is called"
  }, {
    "type" : "list",
    "pdfua_tag" : "L",
    "id" : 9,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 102.6, 286.66, 510.206, 390.307 ],
    "numbering style" : "unordered",
    "number of list items" : 3,
    "list items" : [ {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 102.6, 360.46, 510.206, 390.307 ],
      "font" : "SFRM1200",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "• connected if, for each pair of vertices, there is an undirected path joining those vertices,",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 102.6, 314.62, 510.103, 344.467 ],
      "font" : "SFRM1200",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "• acyclic if it does not contain any directed cycles, i.e. directed paths that visit the same vertex more than once,",
      "kids" : [ ]
    }, {
      "type" : "list item",
      "pdfua_tag" : "LI",
      "page number" : 1,
      "bounding box" : [ 102.6, 286.66, 440.28, 298.627 ],
      "font" : "SFRM1200",
      "font size" : 11.955,
      "text color" : "[0.0]",
      "content" : "• increasing if the labels are decreasing along all directed paths.",
      "kids" : [ ]
    } ]
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.08, 164.019, 510.241, 265.926 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "We observe that if a graph is increasing, then it is acyclic in particular. For v ∈ V , we call a vertex w ∈ V an out-neighbor if (v, w) ∈ E and an in-neighbor if (w, v) ∈ E. Then, the number of out-neighbors of v is denoted by outdeg(v). On the other hand, we call the number of in-neighbors of v the degree of vertex v, denoted by dV (v). For all n, m ∈ N we deﬁne In(m) to be the set of increasing directed graphs on [n] satisfying the condition"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 209.041, 122.783, 510.224, 143.526 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "∀v ∈ [n]: outdeg(v) = m ∧ (v − 1). (2.1)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.081, 98.499, 329.521, 110.467 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Now, we are ready to formally deﬁne RRDAGs."
